---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const moleCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'm', label: 'mass of water (g)', min: 1, max: 100, step: 1, value: 18 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'moles (m ÷ 18.02)', expr: 'm/18.02', unit: 'mol' },
    { label: 'molecules (moles × 6.022e23)', expr: 'm/18.02*6.022e23' },
    { label: 'hydrogen atoms (×2 per molecule)', expr: 'm/18.02*2*6.022e23' },
    { label: 'grams of hydrogen in sample', expr: 'm/18.02*2*1.008', unit: 'g' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/foundational/mole-and-stoichiometry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Imagine a baker who can only count individual grains of flour, and eggs sold only by the
    tonne. That is chemistry's daily predicament: reactions happen molecule-by-molecule, but
    laboratories weigh in grams — and a water molecule (18 atomic mass units) and a
    sucrose molecule (342 u) differ by a factor of nineteen, so equal <em>grams</em> never
    mean equal <em>numbers</em> of particles. The solution is one of the most elegant units
    in all of science: the <strong>mole</strong>. One mole is 6.022 × 10²³ particles
    (Avogadro's number), and — this is the magic — one mole of any substance weighs exactly
    its formula mass in <em>grams</em>. One mole of water is 6.022 × 10²³ molecules and
    weighs 18.02 g; one mole of sodium chloride is the same number of formula units and
    weighs 58.44 g. The mole is the bridge between the invisible counting world and the
    weighable kitchen world, and every quantitative statement in chemistry crosses it.
  </p>
  <p>
    How big is 6.022 × 10²³? If you had a mole of soft drink cans spread over the surface of
    the Earth, they would layer the planet about 320 km deep. It is deliberately, hugely
    enormous — big enough that grams of stuff contain whole numbers of moles. In this lesson
    we build the bridge piece by piece: how formulas encode composition, how relative masses
    are defined, how the mole converts between them, and how balanced equations turn all of
    it into predictions you can test with a scale.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the mole machine — weigh water, count its molecules, and read the atoms hiding inside" widget="sliderLab" config={moleCfg} />

  <p>
    18 g of water is one mole: 6.022 × 10²³ molecules, each with 3 atoms (2 H + 1 O). The
    readouts show why chemists love the unit — mass in, atom counts out, no estimate
    required: 2 moles of hydrogen atoms (12.096 g) plus 1 mole of oxygen atoms (15.999 g)
    reassembles to the 18.02 g you started with. (The number is exact by definition since
    2019: Avogadro's constant is fixed at 6.02214076 × 10²³.)
  </p>

  <h2>Formulae: the notation of composition</h2>
  <p>
    A chemical <strong>formula</strong> is a sentence with three kinds of information packed
    in: which elements, how many of each, and (implicitly) how they bond. H₂O says two
    hydrogen atoms and one oxygen per molecule; the little numbers are <em>subscripts</em>,
    and moving one changes the substance — H₂O₂ is hydrogen peroxide, not "double water".
    <strong>Molecular formulas</strong> name whole molecules (glucose C₆H₁₂O₆);
    <strong>empirical formulas</strong> give the simplest whole-number ratio (glucose's
    empirical formula is CH₂O), and ionic compounds have no molecules at all — NaCl just
    means the 1:1 ratio of the crystal lattice. Formulae scale up into
    <strong>equations</strong>, the sentences of chemistry: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. The
    <em>balancing</em> (equal atoms of each element on both sides) is not bookkeeping
    pedantry — it is the law of conservation of mass written down. Atoms are neither created
    nor destroyed in a reaction, so an unbalanced equation describes an impossible event.
  </p>

  <h2>Relative masses: weighing atoms without weighing atoms</h2>
  <p>
    Atoms are absurdly light — a hydrogen atom is about 1.7 × 10⁻²⁴ g — so chemists long ago
    stopped quoting grams and quote <em>relative</em> masses instead. The
    <strong>relative atomic mass</strong> (A<sub>r</sub>) of an element is its average atomic
    mass compared with the carbon-12 standard, defined as exactly 12. Hydrogen is 1.008,
    carbon 12.011, oxygen 15.999. Two details matter. First, "average": natural carbon is a
    mixture of isotopes (98.9% carbon-12, 1.1% carbon-13), so its A<sub>r</sub> sits slightly
    above 12 — relative atomic masses are isotope-weighted means, which is how mass
    spectrometers "see" an element's isotopic fingerprint. Second, <strong>relative formula
    mass</strong> (M<sub>r</sub>) is just the sum: for water, (2 × 1.008) + 15.999 = 18.02.
    These numbers are dimensionless comparisons — but the mole will shortly make them
    weighable.
  </p>

  <h2>The mole and the Avogadro constant</h2>
  <p>
    The definition is beautiful in its simplicity: <strong>one mole contains exactly
    6.02214076 × 10²³ specified entities</strong>, and the number was chosen precisely so
    that one mole of particles weighs its relative mass in grams. Why does that work?
    Because Avogadro's constant is the number of atomic mass units in one gram (to 10-digit
    precision): 1 u × 6.022 × 10²³ ≈ 1 g. So the M<sub>r</sub> you compute on paper becomes
    the <strong>molar mass</strong> in g/mol you can put on a balance — no conversion factor
    to memorise, because the constant <em>is</em> the conversion. From this, three working
    formulas generate all of stoichiometry:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>moles = mass ÷ molar mass</strong> — 36.04 g of water = 2 mol; 5.85 g of NaCl = 0.1 mol.</li>
    <li><strong>moles = volume ÷ 24 dm³</strong> for any gas at room temperature and pressure — because one mole of any gas occupies ~24,000 cm³ at 20 °C, 1 atm (a consequence of kinetic theory: equal volumes of gas hold equal numbers of particles, Avogadro's own hypothesis). 12 dm³ of any gas ≈ 0.5 mol, whatever it is made of.</li>
    <li><strong>moles = concentration × volume</strong> — a 0.5 mol/dm³ solution contains 0.5 mol per litre, so 250 cm³ of it holds 0.125 mol; the formula behind every titration.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a mole is a weight." It is a <em>count</em> — like a
    dozen, but for particles. Molar mass in g/mol is the bridge that lets the count be
    found from a weighing, not the definition itself. One mole of hydrogen gas (2.016 g) and
    one mole of oxygen gas (31.998 g) contain the same number of molecules and are wildly
    different weights — the whole point.
  </p>

  <h2>Stoichiometry: the recipe arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Stoichiometry</strong> (Greek for "element measuring") is the arithmetic of
    reactions, and it treats a balanced equation as a recipe with three interchangeable
    readings. 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O reads "2 molecules of hydrogen react with 1 of oxygen to make
    2 of water" — and equally, at any scale, "2 moles + 1 mole → 2 moles", because the mole
    counting is the molecule counting scaled up. The ratios of the big coefficients are
    fixed forever, whatever amounts you start with.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example, end to end: how much water is made by burning 8 g of hydrogen in
    unlimited oxygen? Moles of H₂ = 8 ÷ 2.016 ≈ 3.97 mol. The recipe's hydrogen-to-water
    ratio is 2 : 2, so water produced = 3.97 mol. Mass = 3.97 × 18.02 ≈ 71.5 g. (Notice the
    mass grew from 8 g to 71.5 g — the other 63.5 g came from the oxygen, conservation of
    mass audited across two different substances.) Now make it realistic: mix 10 mol H₂
    with 10 mol O₂. The recipe wants 2 : 1, so 10 mol of H₂ needs only 5 mol of O₂ —
    <strong>hydrogen is the limiting reagent</strong>, oxygen is in excess (5 mol left
    over), and the hydrogen sets the yield. Reactants are almost never mixed in exact
    recipe proportions; finding which one runs out first is the first move of every real
    calculation, from industrial synthesis to exam papers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two honesty checks complete the toolkit. <strong>Percent yield</strong> compares what
    you got with what the recipe promised: 45 g of product when theory says 50 g is a 90%
    yield — reactions rarely deliver the maximum, because of side reactions, losses on
    transfer, and (a later lesson) equilibria that stop part-way. <strong>Empirical formula
    from data</strong> runs the machine in reverse: burn an unknown hydrocarbon, weigh the
    CO₂ and H₂O produced, convert each to moles of C and H, and reduce to the simplest
    whole-number ratio — this is literally how the first molecular formulas of the 1800s
    were discovered, and how unknown compounds are still identified today.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more application — <strong>percentage composition</strong> — closes the loop
    between formula and mass. What fraction of iron ore (Fe₂O₃, M<sub>r</sub> = 159.7) is
    actually iron? Iron contributes 2 × 55.8 = 111.7 of the 159.7, so the ore is 69.9%
    iron by mass — the arithmetic that turns a mine's assay report into a business case,
    and the reason a blast furnace's accountant cares about a formula more than a
    molecule. The same arithmetic powers <strong>titration calculations</strong> end to
    end: measure the acid volume that neutralises a measured alkali, convert both to
    moles with concentration × volume, read the mole ratio off the balanced equation,
    and report the unknown concentration. A standard result: neutralising 25.0 cm³ of
    0.100 mol/dm³ NaOH took 23.6 cm³ of H₂SO₄ — moles of NaOH = 2.50 × 10⁻³; the
    equation 2NaOH + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O shows 2 : 1, so acid moles = 1.25 × 10⁻³;
    concentration = 1.25 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.0236 = 0.053 mol/dm³. Notice every number in that
    chain came from one of the three formulas in the list above — the mole machine
    running in the wet lab.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The history clarifies the logic. Avogadro proposed in 1811 that equal volumes of gas
    contain equal numbers of molecules — which immediately explained why 2 volumes of
    hydrogen combine with 1 of oxygen to give 2 of water vapour (molecules must be diatomic:
    H₂ + ½O₂ → H₂O, doubled to clear the fraction). The hypothesis was ignored for fifty
    years, partly because "molecule" and "atom" were used interchangeably; Stanislao
    Cannizzaro's 1860 campaign for it finally fixed the confusion and gave chemistry its
    first reliable atomic masses — the periodic table itself became sortable only after this
    accounting was sound. The mole thus sits at the junction of chemistry's two great
    structure stories: it made the atomic theory quantitative.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quantitatively, two refinements matter at the expert edge. First, gas volumes: the
    24 dm³ figure holds only at ~20 °C and 1 atm; the ideal gas law pV = nRT generalises it
    to any conditions (at 0 °C it is 22.7 dm³), and real gases deviate slightly at high
    pressure — deviations from which van der Waals built corrections, and from which the
    existence of intermolecular forces was first measured. Second, the 2019 SI redefinition:
    the kilogram used to be a platinum–iridium cylinder in a Paris vault, and Avogadro's
    number was measured against it; the SI was redefined to fix the constants instead, so
    the mole is now exact by definition and the kilogram is calibrated <em>from</em> it —
    via the silicon-sphere X-ray crystal density method and the Kibble balance. Chemistry's
    humble accounting unit ended up anchoring the metric system. Titrations — the
    concentration × volume formula meeting an acid–base indicator — are the practical exam
    where all of this becomes wet glassware, and they are covered in the acids and bases
    lesson next door.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (Stoichiometry); BIPM SI redefinition (2019); Cannizzaro, Sketch of a Course of Chemical Philosophy (1860).</p>
</TopicPage>
